<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lesbos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lesbos is a large Greek island in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, close to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with its chief town at Mytilene. Probably the Lazpa of Hittite texts, it was home in the archaic period to the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a> and to the rival cities of Mytilene and Methymna. From 1355 it was ruled by the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gattilusio">Gattilusio family</a> until the Ottoman conquest of 1462, and it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lesbos.jpg" alt="Lesbos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koutrolm · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mytilene_2010-04-03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lesbos lies in the far east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, facing the Turkish coast across the Mytilini Strait, which is about 5.5 kilometres wide at its narrowest. It covers 1,633 square kilometres and has about 400 kilometres of coastline. In the late Palaeolithic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> periods it was joined to the Anatolian mainland. The island is roughly triangular, deeply cut by the gulfs of Kalloni and Gera, and its chief town, Mytilene, stands on the south-east coast. The name may originally have meant forested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived on Lesbos since at least 3000 BCE, with large settlements at Thermi and Lisvori. Two Hittite texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> mention Lazpa, a place usually identified with Lesbos, then apparently a dependent of the Seha River Land. Later Greek writers said that the Penthilidae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> founded Mytilene and ruled it until a revolt of 590 to 580 BCE led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus</a>. Emigrants from Thessaly are believed to have brought the Aeolic dialect, which survives in the poems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a>. In classical times the cities of the island formed a pentapolis of Mytilene, Methymna, Antissa, Eresos and Pyrrha. Pyrrha was destroyed by an earthquake in 231 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> destroyed Antissa in 168 BCE.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the island belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The deposed empress Irene was exiled to Lesbos in 802 and died there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> raided it heavily in the late 9th century, and about 1089 to 1093 the Seljuk emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzachas">Tzachas</a> briefly held it but could not take Methymna. The Gattilusi ruled it as tributary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered it in September 1462.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> moved richer inhabitants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, brought Muslim settlers from Rumelia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and had the cathedral of Mytilene made into a mosque. The island, called Midilli, became a sanjak. Greek naval forces landed on 21 November 1912 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, and the Ottoman commanders surrendered on 21 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lesbos is part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, a regional unit of the North Aegean region with its seat at Mytilene. Its economy rests mainly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, with fishing, soap and ouzo. The whole island is the Lesvos Geopark, a member of the European Geoparks Network since 2000, known for its geological heritage and a petrified forest. Its landmarks include the castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molyvos">Molyvos</a>, Mytilene and Sigri and a Roman aqueduct at Moria.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lesbos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within the Delian League Chios kept its own fleet and full autonomy, and after Athens disarmed Samos and <b>Lesbos</b> it remained the last important ally with that status.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeated rebels paid in land and resources: Thasos lost its land, ships and the mines of Thasos to Athens, and after the revolt of Mytilene the land of <b>Lesbos</b> was redistributed to Athenian shareholders sent to live there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1516 the town’s notables called on the corsair Oruç Barbarossa, a native of <b>Lesbos</b> who had been raiding Christian shipping from Tunisian and Algerian ports, to help them against the Spanish garrison.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek continued to be spoken, dividing into dialects: Ionic and Attic in Athens, the islands and the coast of Asia Minor; Aeolic in <b>Lesbos</b> and Thessaly; Arcadian and Cypriot; and Doric in Sparta, Corinth, Crete and Rhodes.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…combined Greek, Etruscan, Latin and Roman elements: Greek vases of the sixth century BCE already show Aeneas founding a new home in Etruria, he was worshipped at Lavinium, and a fragment usually attributed to Hellanicus of <b>Lesbos</b> made him the founder of Rome with the help of Odysseus.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Irene of Athens, sole ruler of the Byzantine Empire since 797, was overthrown by her General Logothete, Nikephoros, who seized the throne; she was banished to <b>Lesbos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
